  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6db43ff41344f292c8cb5805febd175088fe068fe89927bade0c6fc2246c8781.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
